  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper level trough (ULT) and\nassociated surface boundary in the form of a vigorous cold front\nare currently pushing through the state. Snow levels are dropping\na few hours sooner than modeled so changeover of precipitation\nparticularly along the boundary is occurring sooner than expected\nwhich may increase snowfall amounts especially in central NV. A cross-\nsection of the US Highway 50 corridor is showing signs of strong\nlift combining with instability and moisture through the dendritic\ngrowth zone during the early portions of tomorrow morning. Current\nsnow totals have been adjusted upward but there is concern of \nnarrow banded moderate to heavy snow occurring across a line \nincluding Austin-Eureka-Ely-Great Basin National Park. Snow totals\nare coming in at around one to four inches in these locations, \nbut locally higher amounts of three to seven inches may be \npossible across the higher passes and summits as well as in the \naforementioned locations should a narrow band set up over them.\n\nAnother concern with the frontal passage is brief snow squall \nconditions. Expecting a fairly quick changeover from rain, to rain\nand snow mix, to all snow, and combining with brief strong winds \nas the direction changes to the west and northwest so visibility \nvalues could drop quickly to one-half mile or less in the heaviest\nsnow showers. Finally, there is some weak CAPE and a strong\nfrontogenesis signature along the front that could help to \npromote isolated rumbles of thunder during the morning hours \nTuesday in central Nevada. Further north, amounts along the I-80 \ncorridor continue to be up to a half inch or so, with higher \namounts of one to three inches over the summits and passes on \nI-80. Further north in northern Elko county snow amounts of one to\nfour inches are forecast with the highest amounts likely in the \nJarbidge wilderness. The snow showers will change to a rain and \nsnow mix during the mid-morning hours as high temperatures try to \nrebound into the upper 30s to low 40s. By the end of the \nafternoon, any showers will diminish from west to east with \npartial clearing by late afternoon and evening as a weak shortwave\nridge builds in. Overnight lows will be in the teens to low 20s.\n\nA transitional ridge builds in over the Great Basin Tuesday night\nand most of Wednesday ahead of a colder, stronger system set to\nimpact the region Wednesday night into Thursday. Rain showers over\nwestern NV Wednesday afternoon will push eastward across the state\nby Thursday morning. With snow levels having little time to\nrecover and a system sporting much colder air the impact of\nsnowfall with this second system will be greater across the area.\nCurrent best amounts in the valleys look to be in southern Lander\nand northern Nye counties where 3-6 inches are possible. Further\nnorth along I-80 and in northern Humboldt and Elko Counties snow\namounts with the second system range from T to 2 inches through\nFriday morning. \n\nAnother transitional ridge will build over the region this weekend\nbefore yet another short wave strengthens over the Pacific Ocean\nand impacts the Great Basin early next week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Low confidence remains in QPF\nand snowfall amounts especially in the valleys thanks to continued\npresence of snow squall indicators especially along US-50 corridor\nin central NV. Moderate confidence in second system impacting the\nregion late Wednesday night through Thursday. Better confidence\nexists in below normal temperature trend through the rest of the\nweek.",
